\begin{frame}{Why reinforcement learning in quantum technology?}

Quantum technology contains many sequential decision problems:

\begin{itemize}
    \item choose a control pulse over time;
    \item prepare a target quantum state;
    \item design a high-fidelity gate;
    \item adapt to measurement outcomes;
    \item correct errors before coherence is lost;
    \item optimize sensing protocols under noise.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}

The common structure is

\[
S_t \longrightarrow A_t \longrightarrow (R_{t+1},S_{t+1}).
\]

\vspace{0.2cm}

In quantum applications, the ``environment'' is usually the controlled quantum system, simulator, or experimental device.

\end{frame}
\begin{frame}{What is different from standard RL examples?}

In classical textbook examples, the agent often observes the state directly.

\vspace{0.2cm}

In quantum systems this is usually not true:

\begin{itemize}
    \item the wave function is not directly observable;
    \item measurements are probabilistic;
    \item projective measurements disturb the state;
    \item observations may be only expectation values or bit strings;
    \item rewards are often available only at the end of an episode.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}

Therefore many quantum RL problems are naturally closer to

\[
\text{partially observable, stochastic, finite-horizon control}.
\]

\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Quantum RL definitions}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
RL object & Quantum meaning & Example \\
\hline
State $S_t$ & physical state & $\psi_t$, $\rho_t$ \\
Observation $O_t$ & measured information & $\langle \sigma_z\rangle$, syndrome \\
Action $A_t$ & control operation & pulse, gate, measurement \\
Reward $R_{t+1}$ & task score & fidelity, energy, survival \\
Episode & full protocol & one pulse sequence \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}

The lecture goal is to translate quantum problems into this table.

\vspace{0.2cm}

Once this translation is clear, the choice of algorithm becomes a second-order question.

\end{frame}
\begin{frame}{Three kinds of quantum RL environments}

In quantum technology, the RL environment can mean different things:

\vspace{0.35cm}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Type & Data come from & Main issue \\
\midrule
Simulated & numerical time evolution & model may be imperfect \\
Real & quantum hardware & noisy, slow, partially observed \\
Hybrid & simulation + device data & simulation-to-device transfer \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.45cm}

In all cases, the agent only interacts through the RL loop

\[
O_t \longrightarrow A_t \longrightarrow (R_{t+1},O_{t+1}).
\]

\vspace{0.2cm}

The practical question is: \textbf{what information is actually available to the agent?}

\end{frame}
\begin{frame}{Simulated environment: model-based use}

The RL agent has access to the transition model used to generate the simulated dynamics.

\vspace{0.3cm}

\[
\rho_{t+1}=\mathcal E_{a_t}(\rho_t),
\qquad
|\psi_{t+1}\rangle = U(a_t)|\psi_t\rangle .
\]

\vspace{0.4cm}

\begin{itemize}
    \item planning or gradient-based updates can exploit the model
    \item sample efficiency can improve
    \item transfer to hardware may fail if the model is inaccurate.
    \item following from the last point: realistic models often have different dynamics. Model based training is likely to introduce bias.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}

\end{frame}
\begin{frame}{Simulated environment: model-free use}

A simulated quantum environment uses a physical model to generate transitions.

\[
H(t)=H_0+\sum_k u_k(t)H_k,
\qquad
\rho_t \xrightarrow{A_t} \rho_{t+1}.
\]

\vspace{0.25cm}

But the agent does not need access to the Hamiltonian.

\vspace{0.2cm}

It only receives sampled interaction data:

\[
(O_t,A_t,R_{t+1},O_{t+1}).
\]

\vspace{0.25cm}

\begin{itemize}
    \item the simulator is treated as a black box;
    \item the agent learns from repeated episodes;
    \item useful when we want a policy that does not depend on analytic gradients;
    \item closer in spirit to later deployment on hardware.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.15cm}

\end{frame}
\begin{frame}{Real quantum environments}

On real hardware, the environment is the experimental device itself.

\vspace{0.25cm}

The agent chooses a control,

\[
A_t = \text{pulse, gate, or measurement choice},
\]

and receives only measured information,

\[
O_t = \text{bit string, syndrome, or expectation estimate}.
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}

This is harder than simulation because

\begin{itemize}
    \item the quantum state is not directly observable;
    \item measurement outcomes are stochastic;
    \item measurements can disturb the state;
    \item rewards are often sparse and terminal;
    \item calibration and noise drift over time.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.2cm}


\end{frame}
\begin{frame}{Quantum RL and the Markov assumption}

Quantum RL is Markovian if the state is complete:

\[
S_t=\rho_t,
\qquad
\rho_{t+1}=\mathcal T_{a_t}(\rho_t).
\]

Then the next step depends only on $(S_t,A_t)$.

\vspace{0.3cm}

In experiments, the agent usually sees observations, not $\rho_t$:

\[
O_t =
\langle\sigma_z\rangle_t,
\quad
m_t\in\{-1,+1\},
\quad
\text{syndrome}.
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}

So many quantum RL tasks are better explained by a partially observable Markov decision process:

\[
A_t \sim \pi(\cdot\mid O_1,\ldots,O_t).
\]

\end{frame}
\begin{frame}{First guiding example: state preparation (from week 5)}

Suppose we want to drive

\[
|\psi_0\rangle
\longrightarrow
|\psi_{\mathrm{target}}\rangle .
\]

A simple controlled Hamiltonian may be written as

\[
H(t)=H_0+\sum_k u_k(t)H_k .
\]

At each step, the agent chooses the controls

\[
A_t = (u_1(t),u_2(t),\ldots).
\]

The terminal reward can be the fidelity

\[
R_T =
|\langle \psi_{\mathrm{target}}|\psi_T\rangle|^2 .
\]

\vspace{0.2cm}

This is the simplest bridge from quantum optimal control to RL.

\end{frame}

